API samples for database use

Store each sample with consistent fields for searching, reuse, and testing.

Suggested fields:
- name: brief sample title
- method and endpoint: HTTP verb and URL; use placeholders such as {userId}
- headers and query parameters: JSON objects, excluding secret values
- request_body and response_example: JSON payloads, or null when not applicable
- expected_status: expected HTTP status code
- description, tags, api_version, and last_verified: context and maintenance details

Example record:
{
	"name": "Get user",
	"method": "GET",
	"url": "https://api.example.com/v1/users/{userId}",
	"headers": { "Accept": "application/json" },
	"query_params": {},
	"request_body": null,
	"expected_status": 200,
	"response_example": { "id": "usr_123", "name": "Example User" },
	"description": "Retrieve a user by ID.",
	"tags": ["users", "read"],
	"api_version": "v1"
}

Database notes:
- Keep commonly filtered fields (method, endpoint, status, and tags) searchable; JSON columns work well for headers and payloads.
- Use synthetic data. Never store real API keys, tokens, passwords, or sensitive personal information in samples.
- Document required parameters and authentication without recording secret values.
- Track when examples were last verified and update them when the API changes.
